In article <60564@aerospace.AERO.ORG> srt@aero.UUCP (Scott "TCB" Turner) writes:
>Someone recently posted a spoiler about wielding potions, noting that 
>wielding a potion of blindess would make you blind, for instance.
>
>That's a horrible addition to the game.  First, it doesn't make any
>sense.  Why should wielding a potion give you the effects of the
>potion?  Second, it isn't the kind of thing anyone is likely to try
>unless they are purposely trying all kinds of nonsensical things.

  The reason why it makes no sense is that it is a bug.  However, I have found
  it to be a useful one.  In patchlevel 3, after you wield certain potions, 
  then unwield them, the effect becomes semi-permanent ("invisibility" and
  "levitation" come to mind.)  In patch five, it was fixed to the point where
  the potions only work while wielded.  However, this is still useful.  A) you
  can identify a few potions this way, and B) you can undo sickness and
  blindness with them.  To explain:  You eat rotten food and are blinded.
  Usually, this type of blindness lasts upward of 500 turns.  Wielding a
  potion of blindness also makes you blind.  Unwielding it subsequently 
  removes the blindness -- all blindness.  In the space of three moves you can
  see again!

